er myself  within recollection that is beyond all  possible judgments and i will add beyond  any possible representation right  he says this process of recollection is  not a  looking at something it is a retrieval a  renewal and somebody might say wow  that's that that's really abstract by  itself  what do all these these terms mean i am  grasping  who i am and what i am and what i am  not and what i you might say  am and am not at the same time  through this this process of  recollection in this  state he talks about encountering  oneself in recollection and here  you know marcelo is very very good about  providing examples throughout most of  his works  he doesn't really provide us a lot of  examples here but we might dwell upon  some  and i invite you to think about your own  experiences that  correspond to this the realization  uh in in having regrets that you  not only did something that maybe wasn't  the best option but seemed to be a good  decision at the time but that you broke  a promise  let somebody down let yourself down  didn't turn out to be the kind of person  that you wanted to be that's that's a  possible experience  of recollection where you take a stand  upon yourself  you are at the same time the person  making the judgment and the person  judged we could also do this with things  where  we're more happy about it right thinking  about our successes  but keeping them within perspective  rather than allow it to  rise to some sort of crazy irrational  elation that now thinks everything in  the world is perfect  right we encounter ourselves we get to  know ourselves better  through recollection we never do so as a  complete totality  there's never a moment of total  enlightenment where now we see  everything  as a whole but we do it over and over  and over again  so he also says well this sounds an  awful lot like  the dialectical moment of turning back  to oneself or the physique sign the  being for oneself that is at the center  of german idealism  now if you're not somebody conversant  with german idealism he's talking about  you know  hegel and other thinkers um  marcel himself was very into the german  idealist when he was a younger person  uh he also read the american idealist  royce and did quite a bit of work on him  and he says no it's not the same thing  as that  why he says entering into oneself does  not mean to be  for oneself and to have in view some  sort of intelligible unity of the  subject and the object  instead we are in the presence of the  paradox of mystery  whereby the self into which i return  ceases  precisely to that extent to belong to  itself so he's not saying here that it's  a matter of  lacking self-consciousness or insight  into the self  he's saying what's missing here is the  idea that the self  doesn't just belong to the self that  it belongs to something else it's  connected  with something and he brings up this uh  phrase coming from saint paul you are  not your own  he says this great statement conveys a  significance that is both ontologically  and essentially  concrete it is what best expresses the  reality we're exploring at the moment  you know sometimes we talk about  something being bigger than ourselves  and us belonging to that and that would  be one modality of that  but it could also be the realization  that one doesn't just belong to oneself  but one also belongs to  beings who are dependent upon one  one's children one's students one's  parents one's  spouse one's neighbors it could be  other things as well these do not  exhaust the possibilities  he also considers whether we should call  this an intuition  and he's he's leery about the word  intuition he says  we have to be extremely prudent if we  can talk about an intuition it's an  intuition  that is not or cannot be given as  such the more that an intuition is  central the more it fills up the depth  of the being  that it illumines the less susceptible  it is to to reflect upon itself or to  apprehend itself this is  this is kind of an issue with intuition  it presents itself and he's not worried  so much about the you know purely  subjective side of intuition  hey what if i have different intuitions  than you he's saying intuition just  doesn't actually reflect on itself  instead it's just bam there it is  and it sort of floods you  that's not what recollection is although  we could certainly have  intuitions within recollection like  wow i really screwed up there that could  be an intuition  right um so he goes on and he says  if we reflect beyond that to ask what an  intuition of being might be  we see that it it it's not uh  susceptible to a figure in a collection  to be catalogued  he goes on and he's got a great metaphor  for this to speak of an  intuition of being is to invite us to  play on a silent piano  isn't that a great turn of phrase  something worth thinking about  what does it mean to play on a silent  piano well there's keys there  and you can play all you want but no  sound comes out  why not maybe the the strings are gone  or maybe it's just a magical piano and  there's nothing to come  out of it intuition and the appeal to  intuition and recollection is is like  that  so he says instead of using the term  intuition we should say instead  we have to deal with an once again  assurance  that underlies the development of  thought all  thought even discursive thought he says  from this it follows we can only  approach it through a  movement of conversion that is to say by  here's a important marcelian term  secondary  reflection that reflection through which  i ask myself in which way  and on what basis the initial process  of reflection was possible which  reflection itself postulated the  ontological but  without realizing it and he says this  second reflection  lets recollection become self-conscious  to the extent that recollection  can be thought what when marcel brings  up this term  secondary reflection anytime that we are  sort of detaching ourselves from  our experience and and being that is  reflection by itself it's usually a  first reflection it's analytic  it breaks things down it it separates  things  secondary reflection reunites it's  synthetic and we are  part of it so we're not just this sort  of sit-back  spirit that's looking at all these  things and putting them back together in  a nice  picture we're in the picture we're part  of the picture  and not just us but in our dramatic  situation which means with other beings  as well which makes it rather messy and  complicated and muddy  but he thinks that this is central to  what real  recollection would be so we have  recollection uh assurance  secondary reflection connected together  these are all things that are not things  but rather  processes or uh  acts states that allow us to apprehend  the  mystery or the meta-problematic as such  you
 

